  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nAs high pressure keeps moving over central and northeastern SD \ntoday, it will help to keep showers and storm chances out through \ntonight. Skies will be staying mostly clear through the remainder of \nthe day and into the night, which will help the temperatures tonight \ndrop into the low 40s to low 50s. Humidity will stay fairly high \nthrough the night, and with light winds and fairly saturated soils \nfrom yesterday's rain, there could be patchy areas of fog that \ndevelop over areas east of the Missouri River. This fog will be more \nlocalized and should not go below a mile in visibility.\n\nAn upper-level shortwave will be moving over SD early Wednesday \nmorning into the afternoon. This will help some stronger winds aloft \nto move in central SD tonight. As this low level jet moves over, \nsome warm, moist air moving into central SD will start to develop \nsome isolated to scattered showers and storms early Wednesday \nmorning. As the low level jet moves east through the morning into \nthe afternoon, it will continue to cause these scattered showers and \nstorms over central and northeastern SD. These showers and storms \nwill be fairly weak and will not become severe. Rain amounts from \nthese showers and storms look to not be a lot, with only a 20-40% \nchance for 0.1 inch or more rain to fall mainly over north central \nSD. \n\nThe low-level jet will stick around over central SD through the \nmorning and into the early afternoon. While early in the morning the \nlapse rates will be weaker and it will be harder for the stronger \nwinds to mix down, through the morning and into the afternoon the \nlapse rates will increase, causing stronger winds to get to the \nsurface over central SD. Ensembles show a 40-80% chance for winds to \ngust up around and over 35 mph over areas west of the Missouri \nRiver, and a 30-50% chance for winds to gust up around and over 40 \nmph over Corson and Dewey counties. These winds speeds are strong \nbut they do stay below wind advisory criteria for now. Relative \nhumidity values will stay around and above 50% in the locations and \naround the time of the strongest winds. This helps to keep the fire \nweather concerns lower for Wednesday. \n\nThe next chance for accumulating precipitation will start Thursday \nafternoon through Friday as a warm front and surface low pressure \nmoves in and towards central and northeastern SD. Models are still \nvarying the location of this low and frontal boundary as well as dry \nair moving into northeastern SD Thursday evening into Friday. This \ncauses different outcomes in the location and timing of showers and \nstorms moving through SD. A majority of the models are showing the \nprecipitation moving through mainly south central SD to the east \nover portions of east central and southeastern SD, with the bulk of \nthe rain occurring over eastern SD. However, some models have the \narea of rain more to the north or to the south, leading to lower \nconfidence in the location and some of the timing of the showers and \nstorms. Depending on where the showers and storms end up, as well as \nhow much dry air mixes in, the rainfall amounts vary as well. Some \nmodels have chances for 1-3 inches over central and northeastern SD, \nwith the highest amounts over eastern SD. However, other models keep \nthe values lower, with 0.5-1 inch of rain possible. This is \nsomething to keep an eye on as more model runs come to more of an \nagreement. Either way, there is currently a marginal to slight \nExcessive Rainfall Outlook for portions of central and northeastern \nSD, with the highest chances of flooding potential to the southeast \nof the area.",
